proofs.proof_tex:
Fix a member of \(\mathcal M_n\), and write
\[
 h_a=m_a-g_a,\, \varepsilon_a=Y-g_a(X)\,\text{on }\{D=a\},
 \, e_\lambda=e\vee\lambda.
\]
By ass:right-overlap, \(e\leq1-\eta_+\).  Projection onto the interval \([0,1-\eta_+/2]\), which contains every value of \(e\), is a contraction.  Hence
\[
 |p^\circ-e|\leq|p-e|,\,
 |q_\lambda-e_\lambda|\leq|p^\circ-e|\leq|p-e|,
 \,1-p^\circ\geq\eta_+/2.                                      \tag{1}
\]
All divisions below are therefore legitimate by ass:positivity, \(\lambda>0\), and ass:right-overlap.

The identifying equality \(\tau(P)=E_P[g_1(X)-g_0(X)]\) is the given target functional; it follows from ass:consistency and ass:ignorability and is not used as a definition of the estimator.  Add and subtract the score with true regressions, treated denominator \(e_\lambda\), and control denominator \(1-e\).  Pointwise,
\[
\begin{split}
 \psi_\lambda-\tau(P)
={}&A+B_1+B_0+C_1+C_0+R_1+R_0,\\
 A={}&g_1-g_0-\tau(P)+{D\varepsilon_1\over e_\lambda}
       -{(1-D)\varepsilon_0\over1-e},\\
 B_1={}&h_1\left(1-{D\over e_\lambda}\right),\,
 B_0=-h_0\left(1-{1-D\over1-e}\right),\\
 C_1={}&D\varepsilon_1\left({1\over q_\lambda}-{1\over e_\lambda}\right),\\
 C_0={}&-(1-D)\varepsilon_0
       \left({1\over1-p^\circ}-{1\over1-e}\right),\\
 R_1={}&Dh_1\left({1\over e_\lambda}-{1\over q_\lambda}\right),\,
 R_0=(1-D)h_0\left({1\over1-p^\circ}-{1\over1-e}\right).
                                                               \tag{2}
\end{split}
\]
Conditional residual means vanish by the definitions of \(g_0,g_1\), so \(E_PA=E_PC_1=E_PC_0=E_PB_0=0\).  Also
\[
 E_PB_1=E_P\!left[h_1(X)\left(1-{e(X)\over e_\lambda(X)}\right)\right].
\]
By Cauchy--Schwarz, ass:public-g1-radius, and lem:uniform-shell-calculus,
\[
 |E_PB_1|
 \leq r_{1n}P\{e(X)<\lambda\}^{1/2}
 \leq C r_{1n}\lambda^{\kappa/2}.                              \tag{3}
\]

We next bound the centered empirical terms using only second moments.  Assumptions ass:bounded-treatment-effect-signal, ass:treated-residual-variance, ass:control-residual-variance, and lem:uniform-shell-calculus yield
\[
 \|A\|_{P,2}
 \leq C\left\{1+E_P[(e(X)\vee\lambda)^{-1}]^{1/2}\right\}
 \leq C v_\kappa(\lambda).                                    \tag{4}
\]
Here, explicitly,
\(E[D\varepsilon_1^2/e_\lambda^2]\leq\sigma_1^2E[e/e_\lambda^2]
\leq\sigma_1^2E[e_\lambda^{-1}]\), while the control counterpart is at most \(\sigma_0^2/\eta_+\).  Conditional on \(X\),
\[
 E_P\!\left[h_1^2\left(1-{D\over e_\lambda}\right)^2\middle|X\right]
 \leq h_1^2(1+\lambda^{-1}),
\]
and the analogous control expression is at most \(C h_0^2\).  Thus ass:public-g0-radius and ass:public-g1-radius give
\[
 \|B_1\|_{P,2}\leq r_{1n}\sqrt{1+\lambda^{-1}},
 \, \|B_0\|_{P,2}\leq C r_{0n}.                           \tag{5}
\]
Using (1) and the conditional variance assumptions,
\[
\begin{split}
 E_PC_1^2
 &\leq\sigma_1^2E_P\!\left[{e(p-e)^2\over q_\lambda^2e_\lambda^2}\right]
 \leq {\sigma_1^2r_{en}^2\over\lambda^3},\\
 E_PC_0^2&\leq C\sigma_0^2r_{en}^2.                            \tag{6}
\end{split}
\]
No unassumed product second moment is needed for the remainders: their sample averages are controlled in first moment.  Indeed, (1), Cauchy--Schwarz, and the three public-radius assumptions imply
\[
 E_P|R_1|\leq{E_P(|h_1||p-e|)\over\lambda}
 \leq {r_{1n}r_{en}\over\lambda},\,
 E_P|R_0|\leq C r_{0n}r_{en}.                                 \tag{7}
\]

For i.i.d. sampling, \(E|({\mathbb P}_n-P)Z|\leq\|Z\|_{P,2}/\sqrt n\).  Applying this inequality to (2), using (3)--(7), and absorbing \(r_{0n}/\sqrt n\) and \(r_{en}/\sqrt n\) into \(C n^{-1/2}\) because the radii are at most \(\bar r\), gives the uniform finite-sample inequality
\[
 E_P|\widehat\tau_\lambda-\tau(P)|
 \leq C\left\{
 {v_\kappa(\lambda)\over\sqrt n}
 +{r_{1n}\over\sqrt{n\lambda}}
 +{r_{en}\over\sqrt n\,\lambda^{3/2}}
 +r_{1n}\lambda^{\kappa/2}
 +{r_{1n}r_{en}\over\lambda}
 +r_{0n}r_{en}\right\}.                                     \tag{8}
\]

It remains to verify the advertised tuning rather than declare it optimal.  First suppose the cap in def:oracle-threshold does not bind, and denote the resulting maximum by \(L\).  Since \(L\geq b_{o,n}\),
\[
 {r_{1n}\over\sqrt{nL}}\leq r_{1n}L^{\kappa/2}
 \,\text{because}\, nL^{\kappa+1}\geq1.               \tag{9}
\]
Since \(L\geq b_{e,n}\), \(r_{en}\leq L^{(\kappa+2)/2}\), whence
\[
 {r_{en}\over\sqrt nL^{3/2}}
 \leq {L^{(\kappa-1)/2}\over\sqrt n}
 \leq\begin{cases}
 n^{-1/2}L^{-(1-\kappa)/2},&0<\kappa<1,\\
 n^{-1/2},&\kappa\geq1,
 \end{cases}                                                   \tag{10}
\]
and \(r_{1n}r_{en}/L\leq r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}\).  For \(0<\kappa<1\), \(L\geq b_{g,n}\) gives
\[
 {v_\kappa(L)\over\sqrt n}
 \leq n^{-\kappa/2}r_{1n}^{1-\kappa}\leq Q_\kappa(n,r_{1n}). \tag{11}
\]
For \(\kappa=1\), the same inequality gives
\[
 {v_1(L)\over\sqrt n}
 \leq Cn^{-1/2}\sqrt{1+\log_+(nr_{1n}^2)}=C Q_1(n,r_{1n}),    \tag{12}
\]
and for \(\kappa>1\) this term is \(Cn^{-1/2}\).  Finally, because a maximum raised to the positive power \(\kappa/2\) is at most the sum of its components raised to that power,
\[
 r_{1n}L^{\kappa/2}
 \leq C\left\{r_{1n}n^{-\kappa/[2(\kappa+1)]}
 +r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}
 +\mathbf 1\{\kappa\leq1\}n^{-\kappa/2}r_{1n}^{1-\kappa}\right\}. \tag{13}
\]
The last term is bounded by \(Q_\kappa(n,r_{1n})\), also at \(\kappa=1\).  Equations (8)--(13) prove the bound by \(C\rho_n\) in the nonbinding case.

If the cap binds, at least one active component exceeds the fixed number \(\lambda _0\).  If it is \(b_{o,n}\), then \(n\) is bounded by a class constant and \(\rho_n\geq n^{-1/2}\) absorbs every bounded term in (8).  If it is \(b_{e,n}\), then \(r_{en}\) is bounded below by a positive class constant; hence the two terms proportional to \(r_{1n}\) are absorbed by \(r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}\), while the propensity stochastic term is a fixed multiple of \(n^{-1/2}\).  If \(0<\kappa\leq1\) and it is \(b_{g,n}\), then \(r_{1n}\leq Cn^{-1/2}\), so every term involving \(r_{1n}\) is absorbed by the root-\(n\) part of \(Q_\kappa\).  These cases exhaust cap binding and prove
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}E_P|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|\leq C\rho_n. \tag{14}
\]
The displayed statistic is a measurable function of the observed sample and the supplied functions and radii, so def:estimator-class gives \(\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}\in\mathcal T_n\).  Finally Markov's inequality applied to (14) gives
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|>C\rho_n/\delta\}\leq\delta,
\]
which is the claimed fixed-probability conclusion.

We prove the proposed version for a nonempty model class.  For absolute loss, thm:clipped-absolute-upper and def:absolute-risk give
\[
 \mathfrak R_n^{(1)}
 \leq\sup_{\mathcal M_n}E_P|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|
 \leq C\rho_n.                                                \tag{38}
\]
For \(n\geq5\), the last paragraph of lem:median-risk-upgrade supplies a mean absolute error at most \(K\rho_n\) on each block.  That lemma gives the following display; for \(1\leq n<5\), the same display follows from (15a).  Thus def:root-mse-risk gives
\[
 \mathfrak R_n^{(2)}
 \leq\sup_{\mathcal M_n}\{E_P(\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}-\tau(P))^2\}^{1/2}
 \leq\sqrt{21}K\rho_n.                                      \tag{39}
\]
For fixed \(\delta>0\), Markov's conclusion in thm:clipped-absolute-upper and def:probability-risk imply
\[
 \mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}\leq C_\delta\rho_n. \tag{40}
\]

Conversely, thm:four-channel-converse proves the stronger probability lower bound (37), with probability at least \(2\delta _0\).  Hence, for every \(0<\delta\leq\delta _0\) and every \(u<c\rho_n\), every \(T\in\mathcal T_n\) satisfies
\[
 \sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|T-\tau(P)|>u\}
 \geq\sup_{\mathcal M_n}P\{|T-\tau(P)|\geq c\rho_n\}
 \geq2\delta _0>\delta.                                      \tag{41}
\]
The definition of probability risk gives \(\mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}\geq c\rho_n\).  The absolute and root-mean-squared lower bounds are already consequences of thm:four-channel-converse.  Combining them with (38)--(40) proves all three equivalences, with constants uniform over the stated bounded-radius regime.
